insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at,
  photo_url, photo_alt, photo_credit, photo_source)
values
  ('making-plans-for-after-you-are-gone', 'Making Plans for After You Are Gone', 'The will, the passwords, the medical decisions: the list sits undone for years because every item asks you to picture yourself gone. Why that happens, what is actually on it, and how to get through it in pieces.', 'There is a short list of things people are supposed to sort out, and most of us do not. A will. Who makes decisions if you cannot make them. Where the passwords are. What you want done at the end. The list is not long, and it sits undone for years.

The reason is not laziness. Every item on it asks you to picture yourself gone, and the mind will not hold that picture for long. So you open the drawer, feel something unpleasant, and close it again.

Why it is so easy to put off

Because it is not one task. It looks like one line on a to-do list and it is really six or seven separate errands, some of which need another person.

Because nobody teaches it. Most people have no idea what the documents are called, what they cost, or who you are supposed to ask, and a task you cannot picture the first step of does not get started.

Because superstition gets involved, even in people who do not believe in it. There is a quiet sense that writing it down invites it, which is not true but is extremely common and worth naming rather than arguing with.

And because of the age thing. The list reads as something for old people, so every year you can plausibly say not yet, and the year you cannot say it is usually the year something has already happened.

What tends to happen when people finish it

The part that surprises almost everyone is the relief.

The worry has somewhere to go. Open-ended dread is hard to carry. A folder with the answers in it converts a vague fear into a finished errand, and finished errands stop taking up room.

The people you love are spared the worst version. Families that have to guess at your wishes argue, and the arguments happen at the worst possible moment and last for years. A plain sentence from you ends arguments that would otherwise go on and on.

And the picture gets less frightening with use. The first hour is uncomfortable. The second is noticeably less so. Avoidance keeps a thing at full strength, and contact wears it down.

What is actually on the list

Shorter than it sounds, and most people''s version fits on one page.

A will, which says who gets what and who is in charge of carrying it out. For simple situations this is not expensive, and plenty of people do it with a lawyer in a single appointment.

Someone named to make medical decisions if you cannot, and someone to handle money and paperwork. These are two separate jobs and they do not have to be the same person.

A note on what treatment you would and would not want at the end. This is the one people skip and the one families most need.

Where things are. The accounts, the policies, the deed, the password manager. A single sheet saying where to look is worth more than most of the documents.

What you want at the funeral, if you have a view. Burial or cremation, music, who you want told.

And who to call. A short list of names and numbers, because the people who have to make the calls often do not know each other.

Doing it in small pieces

The whole thing in one sitting is how it stays undone. Pick the smallest piece.

Write the where-things-are sheet first. It takes twenty minutes, needs nobody''s help, and is the single most useful item on the list.

Then make one appointment. The appointment is the hard part, and it is also the part that puts the rest on a schedule that is not yours to keep postponing.

Give it a boring slot. A Tuesday evening, an hour, with something ordinary planned afterward. Doing this on a quiet Sunday with nothing after it is how people end up sitting in it.

And accept a rough version now over a perfect one later. A simple will is enormously better than an intention, and you can change it whenever you like.

Telling people

The documents do half the work. The conversation does the other half, and it goes better than people expect.

Keep it short and unceremonious. I have written it down, it is in this drawer, you are the one I named. Three sentences.

Do not spring it as a big announcement. People hear a dramatic opening and start looking for bad news.

And tell the person you named before they find out from a document. Being named is a job, and it is fair to let someone say that they would rather not do it.

If the worry does not stop afterward

For most people, finishing the list quiets things down. For some it does the opposite, and the paperwork becomes one more thing to keep checking and revising.

That is worth noticing. If you are rereading the documents, redoing them, or finding that thoughts about dying now arrive more often rather than less, the planning has stopped being practical and turned into something the worry is feeding on.

A therapist can help with that directly, and it is well-trodden ground. The same is true if the list has become impossible to look at, or if thinking about the end of your life has started to feel less like planning and more like something you want. Say that out loud to a doctor or a therapist rather than carrying it alone.', array['Life Transitions', 'Worry']::text[], 0, '2026-11-21T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/making-plans-for-after-you-are-gone.jpg', 'A fountain pen lying on a page of handwriting', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/3305216/free-photo-image-pen-cc0-creative-commons'),
  ('when-you-keep-going-back-to-check-the-door', 'When You Keep Going Back to Check the Door', 'The lock was locked and you still are not sure. Why going back to check makes the doubt worse rather than better, and the unglamorous method therapists use to shrink it.', 'You locked it. You remember locking it. You are halfway down the street and the certainty has drained out, and now there is a choice between walking back or spending the morning with a low hum of doubt.

So you walk back. The door is locked. There is about four minutes of relief, and then the whole thing starts again tomorrow.

What checking actually does to certainty

This is the part worth understanding, because it is the opposite of what common sense predicts.

Checking feels like it should build confidence. One more look, and you will know. In practice, repeated checking makes the memory worse rather than better.

The reason is that the twentieth look at a lock is not a vivid experience. It is a blur of nearly identical moments, and a blur is hard to retrieve clearly later. The more times you have checked something, the less distinct any one of those checks becomes.

So you end up with a strong feeling of doubt and a weak memory to answer it with, which is exactly the combination that sends you back to the door.

Why certainty is the wrong target

Most people are trying to reach a feeling: I know it is locked. That feeling is not available on demand, and chasing it is what turns a ten-second task into a morning.

What is available is evidence. You turned the handle, you heard it, the door did not open. That is a fact, and it stays true whether or not it feels convincing.

The useful move is to accept the gap between the two. The door is locked and I do not feel sure is a complete and accurate sentence, and learning to leave the house inside it is most of the work.

Breaking the loop in practice

The method therapists use for this is unglamorous and it works. It is not willpower and it is not reassurance.

Check once, deliberately, and pay attention while you do it. One slow check with your attention on it is worth more than six fast ones, because it leaves a memory you can actually find later.

Then say what you did out loud. I locked the door at eight ten. A spoken sentence is far easier to retrieve than a wordless action.

Then leave, and do not go back. This is the hard instruction and it is the one that changes things. The doubt will rise, peak, and come down on its own within twenty minutes or so, and every time you let it do that without going back, the next one is slightly smaller.

Expect it to feel worse before it feels better. The first few times you walk on, the anxiety is higher than usual, because the thing that used to switch it off is no longer available. That spike is the process working, not a sign that you have done something reckless.

Things that look like help and are not

Taking a photograph of the lock. This works for about a week and then becomes the new thing you check, often repeatedly, and now you are checking a picture.

Asking someone else whether they locked it. The relief comes from the other person, which means the doubt now needs them, and that is a harder loop to climb out of than the original one.

Doing it a set number of times, or in a particular order. Rules like that feel like control and they add structure to the thing you are trying to shrink.

And lying awake running back through the evening to find the moment you locked it. The memory search is checking in a different form, and it has the same effect.

What makes it worse, reliably

Tiredness, because a tired brain lays down weaker memories and you will have less to work with.

Doing it on autopilot while talking or carrying things, which is why so much of this happens at the front door with a bag in each hand.

Rushing, which guarantees an unmemorable check.

And stress in general. Checking tends to climb during a hard month and ease off afterward, which is worth knowing so you do not read a bad week as a permanent change.

When to take it to someone

A certain amount of this is ordinary and most people do some version of it.

It is worth talking to a professional when it is costing you time you cannot spare, when you are late or avoiding leaving, when the checking has spread to the stove, the windows, the car and the faucets, or when the doubt arrives with a frightening picture of what would happen attached to it.

That last one in particular responds well to treatment, and the treatment is practical and usually short: a therapist helps you cut the checking down in steps while the doubt is allowed to be there, and the certainty you were chasing turns out not to have been necessary. Your doctor can refer you, and it is a common reason to ask.', array['Anxiety', 'CBT']::text[], 0, '2026-11-21T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-keep-going-back-to-check-the-door.jpg', 'Keys left in the lock of an open front door, with greenery outside', 'Photo by WDnet Studio via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/keys-door-Z1TKDI29FZ'),
  ('when-you-were-taught-to-clear-your-plate', 'When You Were Taught to Clear Your Plate', 'A childhood rule about empty plates can still be running your meals decades later. Where it came from, why eating food you do not want does not prevent waste, and how to loosen it a little at a time.', 'You were not allowed to leave the table until the plate was empty. Or there was a rule about waste, or a story about what someone else did not have, or somebody sat with you until the last of it was gone.

Thirty years later you still finish what is in front of you, whether or not you want it, and leaving food on a plate produces a small flinch that is out of all proportion to a few forkfuls of rice.

Where the rule came from

It usually came from somewhere reasonable, which is part of what makes it hard to put down.

Money, most often. In a house where food was counted, throwing any of it away was a real loss, and the rule was a sensible response to a real situation.

History, sometimes. A grandparent who went without, a war, a move, a lean decade. The rule travels down through families long after the conditions that made it necessary are gone.

Or it was about something other than food. Finishing the plate was obedience, or gratitude, or proof that the cooking was appreciated, and the food was simply where that got measured.

Whatever the source, you learned it before you could evaluate it, which is why it does not respond to being told that it is no longer necessary.

What it does now

The obvious effect is that you eat past the point of wanting to, which is uncomfortable for an hour and tends to put you off the next meal.

The less obvious one is that you have lost a signal. If the plate decides when you stop, you stop paying attention to the other thing that could have decided it, and that sense gets quieter the longer it goes unused.

It also makes other people''s cooking a strain. A large portion at someone''s house becomes a task to complete rather than a meal, and most of your attention goes to the plate instead of the table.

And it tends to come with guilt either way. Finishing when you did not want to feels bad, and leaving it feels worse, which is a trap with no comfortable exit.

Separating waste from eating

This is the move that helps most, because the rule bundles two different things together.

Not wasting food is a good thing to care about. Eating food you do not want does not prevent waste. It routes it through you, which helps nobody, and the food is gone either way.

The real answer to waste is at the other end: smaller portions served, leftovers kept, things used up before they turn. Those actually reduce it. An empty plate does not.

Try saying it plainly to yourself. This food is already made. Eating it when I am full does not save anything. That sentence is doing more work than it looks like.

Practicing the small version

You do not start by leaving half a dinner. Start somewhere that barely counts.

Serve yourself less than you think you want, and go back if you are still hungry. Going back is allowed, and it is much easier than stopping.

Leave something deliberately once. Three bites, on purpose, and sit with the discomfort rather than talking yourself out of it. It passes in a few minutes and it gets easier every time.

Put the leftovers away in front of yourself. Seeing the food saved rather than scraped into the trash answers the waste part directly, which is where most of the flinch is coming from.

And slow one meal a week down enough to notice halfway through. Not every meal. One is enough to get the signal working again.

Eating at other people''s tables

This is the hardest setting, because the rule was a family rule, and families are where it gets enforced.

Ask for less at the start rather than leaving it at the end. A small plate, please. Half of that. This is easy to say and it avoids the whole problem.

Have one sentence ready for the pressure. That was really good and I am full. Say it warmly and then stop explaining, because a second sentence invites a negotiation.

And accept that some people will not like it. A host who is upset by a half-eaten plate is usually not upset about the food, and that is theirs to carry rather than yours to fix with your appetite.

When it is worth bringing to someone

Habits like this are ordinary and plenty of people shift them on their own.

It is worth talking to a professional if the thought of leaving food is genuinely distressing, if eating has become something you plan around and feel bad about most days, if the rules about what and how much you are allowed keep getting tighter, or if you find yourself eating large amounts and then trying to undo it.

A therapist who works with eating can help with this, and so can a registered dietitian for the practical side. Your doctor can point you to both, and it is a reasonable thing to ask about.', array['Food', 'Family']::text[], 0, '2026-11-21T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-were-taught-to-clear-your-plate.jpg', 'Empty white bowls and wine glasses set out on a dining table', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6038806/photo-image-public-domain-table-free'),
  ('when-someone-keeps-canceling-on-you', 'When Someone Keeps Canceling on You', 'A pattern of last-minute cancellations costs more than the evenings it takes. What is usually behind it, how to name the pattern without a confrontation, and what to change on your own side.', 'The message comes two hours before. Something has come up. So sorry. Can we do next week.

It is the fourth time. You say of course, no problem, and then sit with an evening you had cleared and a feeling you cannot quite name, somewhere between disappointed and embarrassed for having looked forward to it.

Why it lands harder than one evening

A single cancellation is nothing. A pattern is a different thing, and it is worth being clear about why.

You paid twice. Once in the time you held open, and again in the looking forward, and the second one is the part nobody counts.

It reads as a ranking. Whatever the real reason, repeated cancellation says that something arrived which was easier to say yes to, and it is hard not to hear that as where you sit on a list.

It stops you making other plans. After a few rounds you start half-booking, keeping things loose, not inviting anyone else, and the cost spreads well past the evenings that got canceled.

And there is the embarrassment, which is the worst of it. Having been visibly excited about something that did not happen feels exposing, and that feeling is what pushes people into pretending they did not mind.

What is usually going on

Worth running through, because the explanation changes what you should do.

Most often it is overcommitting rather than dishonesty. Some people say yes to everything with real enthusiasm and then cannot deliver, and they feel terrible about it each time without ever changing the yes.

Sometimes it is a life that genuinely cannot hold plans. Small children, night work, caring for someone, illness. The cancellations are real and the person is not choosing them.

Sometimes it is avoidance. Social plans can be frightening, and canceling at the last minute is what that looks like from the outside.

And occasionally it is what it appears to be. The friendship matters less to them than it does to you, and they are not going to say so.

You often cannot tell which from where you are standing, which is why guessing is a poor use of your attention.

Saying something, without making it a confrontation

The conversation most people dread is far smaller than they imagine, and not having it is what turns this into resentment.

Name the pattern, not the last instance. Lead with the latest cancellation and you will get an explanation for that one and nothing changes. I have noticed we keep having to rearrange is the sentence that gets at the real thing.

Say what it costs you, plainly and briefly. I end up keeping evenings free and then not making other plans. That is information rather than an accusation, and most people respond to it.

Ask for something specific. Can we put one thing a month in and treat it as fixed. Would it help to do something shorter. Is there a day that is genuinely easier for you.

And leave the door open to the real answer. If this is a bad stretch for you, tell me and I will stop asking for a while. That sentence gives an overloaded person a dignified way out, and it is often what they needed.

Changing what you put in

Whatever they say, there is a piece here that is entirely yours, and it is the piece that makes the difference.

Stop holding evenings open for a plan that has not firmed up. Make the other arrangement. If the plan survives, good. If not, you have not lost the night.

Suggest things that cost you less to lose. A walk, a coffee, something nearby, something you can do alone if they drop out. Elaborate plans give a cancellation more to take away.

Be the one who confirms the day before. Not as a test, simply as a habit. It saves everybody the two-hour-notice version.

And stop pretending you did not mind, at least to yourself. I was looking forward to that and I am annoyed is accurate, and swallowing it is what turns into coldness six months later.

Deciding what this friendship is

Sometimes the honest conclusion is that this is a person you enjoy and cannot rely on, and that is a real category rather than a failure.

You can keep them and stop lending them your calendar. See them when it happens, suggest things with low stakes, and put the part of you that wants a dependable standing arrangement somewhere it will be met.

And if your whole social life is running through one or two people who keep dropping out, the cancellations are not really the problem. Thin ground is, and the answer is a wider base rather than a better conversation with this one friend.

When it is more than this

If the evenings alone have stretched into most of them, if you have stopped suggesting anything because you expect to be let down, or if this has you concluding that you are not worth showing up for, that is worth taking to a therapist.

Loneliness is one of the most workable things people bring to counseling, and the work is practical: what you are reading into the pattern, what you have stopped doing, and how to build something that does not rest on one unreliable yes. Your doctor can tell you what is available locally if you are not sure where to start.', array['Relationships', 'Loneliness']::text[], 0, '2026-11-21T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-someone-keeps-canceling-on-you.jpg', 'A cup and saucer beside a small vase of flowers on a wooden counter', 'Photo by Burst via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/cafe-window-NDIQFBYIHR'),
  ('when-your-hobby-starts-feeling-like-a-job', 'When Your Hobby Starts Feeling Like a Job', 'The thing you did for fun now has a backlog, an audience and a deadline. How that conversion happens, what it quietly takes away, and how to fence off a part of it that stays yours.', 'It used to be the thing you did on a Sunday morning because you wanted to. Now there is a backlog, a page with followers on it, an order to fill, a set of people who ask when the next one is coming, and a faint dread when you walk into the room where it lives.

Nothing went wrong, exactly. It got better. And in getting better it picked up all the features of work, so the one thing in your week that was not an obligation has become one.

How a hobby turns into work

It almost never happens in one step, which is why it is hard to notice.

Someone asks if you would make one for them, and you say yes because it is flattering. Now there is a deadline.

You get good enough that other people have opinions. Once there is an audience, the question quietly changes from do I want to do this to is this good enough to show.

You start measuring. Numbers, orders, personal records, the sheet of what you finished this month. Measuring is useful for improving and it is corrosive for enjoying.

And it starts paying, even a little. Money is the strongest converter of all. Something you are paid for cannot easily be done badly or abandoned halfway, and doing it badly and abandoning it halfway is most of what a hobby is for.

What you actually lost

Naming this matters, because people try to fix the wrong thing.

You lost the right to be bad at it. The whole value of an amateur activity is that nothing rides on the result, and that disappears the moment someone is waiting.

You lost the open end. Something you can stop in the middle of, with no plan, is restful in a way that a project with a finish line is not.

You lost the privacy. Doing something only you see is a different experience from doing it in front of people, even kind people, and the shift is larger than it sounds.

And you lost the part of your week that was not for anything. Everything else you do has a purpose. The point of this was that it did not.

Taking some of it back

You do not have to give up the audience or the income. You do have to separate the two activities, because they are two activities now.

Draw the line somewhere. One day a week, or one project, or one notebook that nobody sees. The rule is that this part is never shown, sold, finished or counted.

Deliberately make something bad in the protected part. On purpose, badly, and throw it away. This feels absurd and it is the quickest way to get the old thing back.

Stop measuring the protected part. No photographs, no posting, no log. If you are tracking it, it is work.

And put a floor under the commitments rather than a ceiling on them. One order a month, not as many as I can manage. A ceiling gets raised. A floor holds.

If you want to stop for a while

A break is not quitting, and it is usually the thing people most need and most resist, because they have made the hobby part of who they are.

Say a plain version out loud. I am taking three months off. Nobody is owed an explanation, and a specific length is easier for everyone than a vague drifting away.

Expect to feel strange rather than relieved at first. When something has been structuring your weeks, removing it leaves a hole before it leaves room, and the room takes a few weeks to arrive.

And consider that you might be done, which is allowed. Interests have lifespans. Having loved something for six years and not wanting it anymore is not a failure of character, and forcing it is how people end up unable to enjoy anything.

When the dread is bigger than the hobby

Worth checking the direction of travel here, because this often runs the other way.

If everything has flattened out, not only this, if things you used to look forward to all feel like obligations now, that is not really about the hobby, and treating it as a scheduling problem will not touch it.

The same goes if the dread arrived alongside a hard stretch at work, a loss, or a long run of exhaustion. The hobby is often the first thing to go and the last thing to come back, which makes it a decent early signal rather than the cause.

And if the measuring has spread, so that most things you do now get scored and most of the scores feel like a verdict on you, that is the thing worth addressing rather than any one activity.

A therapist can help with either of those, and both are common ground. If the flatness has lasted weeks, or you are getting no pleasure out of anything at all, bring it to your doctor as well, because that is worth looking at properly rather than waiting out.', array['Motivation', 'Burnout']::text[], 0, '2026-11-21T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-your-hobby-starts-feeling-like-a-job.jpg', 'Paint brushes standing upright in a plastic cup', 'Photo by Negative Space via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/paint-brushes-265GAALCWG')
